well this one has certainly been a long  time coming we've known about the Dead  Space remake for a while now but outside  of some developer live streams showing  off early footage and that recent  trailer we haven't seen all that much  concrete gameplay that all changed a few  weeks ago when press got Hands-On with  the first three chapters of the game  equating to about three hours and boy is  there a lot to talk about personally I  haven't really been sure how to feel  about this remake going in I think the  original Dead Space is a masterpiece one  of the finest horror games you can play  today and despite its age I think the  art Direction holds up and it still  looks really good honestly it's harder  to see a dead space remake needing to  exist in the same way as games like  Resident Evil 2 and Final Fantasy VII  remake which so thoroughly redefined  their respective Originals both of which  are over two decades old so I went into  this thing kind of wondering how it's  going to justify its existence after  playing this remake I wouldn't say my  mind has completely changed on that  front I don't know if a dead space  remake needs to exist however I did have  a really excellent time with this game I  think EA motive has nailed the look and  feel of dead space and are bringing a  bunch of changes both big and small to  help create a fresh experience I was  super bummed when I hit the end of  chapter 3 because I just kind of wanted  to keep going and on a whole it has me  feeling really excited about dead space  again something I wasn't sure I would  feel again after the disappointment of  dead space 3. so if that's the goal of  this remake to revitalize the franchise  and remind people why Dead Space is so  damn good then I think it's going to be  a huge success so why don't we just Dive  Right In shall we  foreign  but first up gotta let you know that  while we did play the first three  chapters we could only capture chapters  two and three which is why I don't have  footage of the remakes chapter 1 to show  you so you might be wondering what type  of remake is this  really reductive for a moment and create  a line graph featuring video game  remakes on one end I'd have a game like  The Last of Us Part One which features a  complete visual overhaul but is  otherwise the same game we all remember  the other end of the spectrum would be  something like Final Fantasy VII remake  which completely reimagines The Story  characters and gameplay the Dead Space  remake sits somewhere in the middle at  its core based on these opening chapters  the game follows the same beats as the  original the level design is mostly the  same and at least so far the story is  the same but within that familiar  framework changes are made to either  update certain elements to match modern  game design or provide unexpected  wrinkles to the gameplay in order to  freshen up the experience for long time  fans for example our boyars at Clark  talks now they're out of fuel the  centrifuge is offline or Tethered to a 4  trillion ton payload without the engines  it's dragging us down to the planet can  you handle it alone  sure fix the centrifuge get the fuel  running then do a full restart this is  undoubtedly going to be a point of  contention for some people even back  when Isaac got his voice in Dead Space 2  there was a large part of the audience  that didn't care for it preferring the  silent Isaac of the original I think  there's a strong argument to be made for  both cases but for me personally I  actually really like that he talks now  it's not like they turned him into a  never-ending quip machine he just  engages in the conversations when people  call him it just feels more natural to  have him actually respond to people  talking to him or expressing a  personality at all such as concern for  the whereabouts of his girlfriend Nicole  it worked in the original when we didn't  know anything about Isaac and he was  kind of a blank slate but now that we've  had two games with him as an established  character I think it would be weird to  go back to him being silent it really  helps that Isaac's voice actor returns  for the role making the remix Isaac feel  more connected to the Isaac of Dead  Space 2 and 3. and it's not just Isaac  the rest of dead space's small cast of  characters has been updated with new  faces and voice actors The Script  follows the overall same structure but  lines have been Rewritten resulting in  characters like Hammond for example  having a slightly different personality  back in the original game Hammond kind  of came across as a hard ass barking  orders and trying to keep the mission  going despite how [ __ ] everything was  we can still do this get me the  captain's rig codes and we'll find  Nicole but in the remake he feels more  like a guy trying his best to keep a  level head and hold it together Isaac  have you heard from Daniel we were  attacked she ran the other way in turn  Kendra isn't immediately as hostile and  distrusting towards Hammond in the  beginning audio logs have also seen  changes and in some cases outright  change to be completely different it's  hard to judge right now how these  changes will shake out I don't think the  original script and dialogue was bad but  it certainly wasn't the best part of the  game either so I'm all for motive  choosing to update it the real question  is whether more serious deviations will  happen in later chapters of course a big  part of a remake is its visuals and holy  heck this game is gorgeous I mean just  look at it everything in the game has  seen some sort of update and some of the  changes are pretty mind-blowing the most  exciting part about this is not just  that it looks nice though it's that  these modern visuals can mix up and  enhance the horror career the game is a  lot darker overall and that means more  opportunities to show off new lighting  techniques for example I really love how  the shots on your weapon now light up  the space around them creating this  really intense feel I mean just compare  how it looks here with the pulse rifle  in Dead Space original versus how it  looks in the remake  [Applause]  something really important though is  that I think EA motive has nailed the  aesthetic of the original remakes can  often struggle to maintain the  original's looking tone going too far or  ending up feeling uncomfortably  different not the case here I just  thought oh yeah sure this looks better  but this is what I picture when I think  of dead space It wasn't until I got home  and compared the two that I realized how  much was actually that different one of  the changes I quite liked was the  inclusion of circuit breakers next to a  lot of the power socket puzzles when you  place the um Power rectangle yeah let's  let's go with that when you place the  power rectangle into the power socket  the breaker will light up you then have  to choose where power is diverted to the  first one you come across teaches you  the concept by having you pick between  powering a door or an elevator then a  later example requires you to turn on  the power to a console but doing so you  have to make the choice between turning  off the lights  or the oxygen  the Remake also lifts the control scheme  and mechanics from Dead Space 2 and  plops it in here  that means snap your controls using the  B button as a health pack and stomp it  on enemies to collect their goodies  something I actually forgot wasn't in  the original you might think how this  affects the pacing of dead space but  because the Remake was built around  these updates they kind of compensate  the enemies for these changes so I found  the pacing and rhythm of combat to feel  pretty much the same I think it still  has that slower horror Pace that the  original Dead Space is known for and  that's a good thing same goes with the  weapons which all feel good and the way  you expect an interesting change is that  you'll actually find some of the weapons  naturally as you play rather than  collecting their Blueprints and  purchasing them at the shop for example  I found the flamethrower right before  entering the area in chapter 3 where the  walls are covered in flesh and they  start throwing these little Annoying  guys that are best handled with you know  the flamethrower I'm curious if this is  going to be the case with every weapon  or if there will still be ones you pick  up blueprints for blueprints for ammo  and other items are still a thing so  it's not like you aren't doing that as  well so you might have seen the  developers talk about how the ishimura  is now a more seamless map with no  loading times and you can walk from one  end of the ship all the way to the other  and when I first heard this I thought to  myself well that's neat but how does  that actually play out the original game  is separated very cleanly from chapter  to chapter and you weren't even able to  travel back to previous areas well it  turns out in this version of Dead Space  the good old ishimura transit system is  a bit busted now which means instead  they've added these new sections to help  bridge chapters for example when  traveling from chapter one to two you  take the service tunnel to get into the  medical Bay or for chapter 3 you  actually have to head back to the hangar  and travel through a new zero gravity  area to get into engineering I think  this is a great change not only is it a  neat way to help bridge the game  together as a seamless experience it  also kind of breaks up the  repetitiveness of using the tram at the  beginning and end of every level now  that said and once you unlock the train  you can use it at any time to return to  previous locations it's unclear to what  extent you want to return to previous  areas but one reason might be for the  new security locks you'll find doors and  lockers with a security level and during  certain story beats you'll unlock higher  tiers and then you can go back to these  locked rooms if you want to collect  extra resources these locks are  basically replacements for the original  node lock doors in the original where  you had to waste a power node to open up  a door and well hope that that door had  good stuff to find because otherwise hey  you wasted a power node that means in  this version backtracking is more linked  to narrative progression I'm not sure  entirely how I feel about this I did  sort of like the risk reward of using a  power node and hoping you got something  good but as long as it's balanced right  I think it's a fine change another  reason to return to previous areas might  be for side missions that's right Dead  Space 1 has optional Quest now based on  what we've played these seem to be  pretty sparring as a result only one  side mission we encountered in the first  three chapters what's interesting is  that this mission was actually about  Nicole Isaac's girlfriend when he  visited the medical Bay the place where  Nicole worked Isaac naturally brings up  wanting to find her prompting the side  mission this basically means going  slightly off the beaten path to find  hologram recordings of her in an attempt  to discover what happened to her this  Sparks a scavenger hunt that essentially  encompasses the entire ship I love the  idea of using small side objectives to  help flesh out and see Nicole early in  as a character which ties really well to  Isaac having a personality now I'm very  curious to see how many of these  optional side missions there are in the  game and how the potential for  backtracking affects the overall pacing  I'm feeling really good about what I've  played of this remake it's pretty clear  EA motive gets what makes Dead Space  such a classic and I think they're  treating the changes of a lot of care  and respect at least in these first  three chapters intriguingly when fellow  game spotter Jordan Renee interviewed  the developers they didn't bring up  events from the final four chapters that  covers the part of the game on The Valor  all the way up into the game's final  conclusion for as much as I've praised  the original as a masterpiece this whole  time I gotta be honest the final few  chapters are big stinkers for me it's a  huge drop in both storytelling and  gameplay quality and the conclusion is  really lackluster while this is just  speculation on my part I think the fact  that the developers aren't sharing what  the final chapters of the Remake are  like implies a much greater level of  change for the rest of the game that's  something I would personally really like  to see and could be the thing that makes  this remake go from a good update to the  original to something that could even  surpass the original maybe hopefully  we'll just have to wait and see for  ourselves when the game comes out on  January 27th and hey if you're dying to  know even more about dead space after  watching this video I highly recommend  heading over to gamespot.com and reading  Jordan remains Impressions he's got his  own take on the Remake plus some insight  from the developers link is in the  description

